WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Legacy React Native had a persistent performance bottleneck.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.200
Every piece of data passing between JavaScript and the Native OS

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.000
had to be serialized into a JSON string,

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.300
shoved across an asynchronous bridge,

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.200
and parsed on the other side.

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.900
That model is obsolete in the new architecture.

7
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.800
We are no longer passing messages back and forth.

8
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.800
Instead, we are using direct synchronous memory references.

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.600
This happens at the JSI or JavaScript interface boundary.

10
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.200
JavaScript variables now hold physical pointers directly

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.200
to C++ objects resting in memory.

12
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.500
React Native uses CodeGen to parse TypeScript specs,

13
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.500
writing the exact C++ dispatch code.

14
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.800
Your TypeScript functions as a rigid C++ blueprint,

15
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.200
structuring physical native memory.

16
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.100
Old bridge mismatches caused silent crashes.

17
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.500
Now CodeGen enforces this contract,

18
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.500
turning mismatches into instant CI failures.

19
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.200
By treating TypeScript as a strict C++ contract,

20
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.400
you eliminate runtime volatility

21
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.700
and guarantee cross-platform memory safety

22
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.400
before the app ever reaches a device.

23
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.700
Let's look at how basic primitive types,

24
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.900
strings, bullions, and numbers

25
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.000
are mapped directly through this new system

26
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.200
without allocation overhead.

27
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.900
Strings offer a distinct performance advantage across the boundary.

28
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.400
The Hermes engine natively handles transmission

29
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.200
using an efficient zero-copy mechanism.

30
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.600
Looking at this chart, a JavaScript string

31
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.160
points directly to a JSI string in C++ space,

32
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.100
skipping memory duplication.

33
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.300
For numbers, every JavaScript number

34
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.700
maps directly to a standard double primitive

35
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.300
at the C++ level requiring zero allocation.

36
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.400
Morning tone, but there is a strict constraint.

37
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.200
Numbers cannot be nullable within a turbo-module specification.

38
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.700
Sometimes a standard double isn't precise enough

39
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.900
for the native side.

40
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.800
You might require an exact 32-bit integer.

41
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.200
To enforce this precise intent,

42
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.900
you must explicitly import int32, float,

43
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.400
or double directly from the code gen types package.

44
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.700
You need to be aware of a specific bug.

45
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.000
Issue 45659, affecting certain react native versions

46
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.100
where int32 mistakenly generates Java doubles

47
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.600
instead of integers.

48
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.800
To work around this, run the dot-slash-gradle-you-generate

49
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.100
code gen artifacts from schema command

50
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.300
and manually inspect the generated output

51
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.800
to ensure your types align.

52
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.800
Knowing exactly how these primitives map into memory

53
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.900
prevents accidental allocations

54
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.100
and stops unintended data truncation on the native side.

55
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.300
For complex data structures, generic untyped object

56
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.300
definitions are deprecated in new turbo-module code.

57
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.600
You must use strict typed object literals instead.

58
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.200
Code Gen uses this explicit TypeScript shape

59
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.500
to generate C++ code that prevalidates the structure,

60
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.300
extracting exact fields by name right at the C++ layer.

61
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.300
Once extracted in C++, a data is packed

62
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.900
into the appropriate OS specific structure,

63
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.500
a readable map for Android, or an end-estictionary for iOS

64
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.900
before reaching your native implementation.

65
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.700
Rapping these object literals in the read-only utility type

66
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.200
is the preferred standard.

67
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.400
It communicates strict immutability to the native environment.

68
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.000
Arrays follow a similarly rigid boundary rule.

69
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.200
They cross the JSI boundary exclusively

70
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.700
as read-only references.

71
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.300
The physical consequence is that any mutation performed

72
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.200
on an array within the native OS layer

73
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.500
will not reflect back to the JavaScript layer.

74
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.700
To update array values, you must instantiate

75
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.600
an entirely new array on the native side

76
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.400
and return it across the boundary.

77
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.000
Enforcing structural immutability at the TypeScript level

78
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.900
aligns with how JSI manages complex memory references

79
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.000
without causing synchronization errors.

80
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.600
This migration introduces a specific friction point.

81
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.800
Valid application TypeScript patterns

82
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.500
will trigger hard build failures

83
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.800
when used in turbo-modules back.

84
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.700
Take a look at this comparison.

85
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.200
Utility types like partial and generics

86
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.800
are rejected when mapping to fixed C++ structs.

87
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.400
Co-Gen operates purely as a parser.

88
00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.700
It is not a full TypeScript language server,

89
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.500
meaning it lacks the capability to compute and resolve

90
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.300
utility expansions.

91
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.400
Generics and tuples fail validation

92
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.400
for similar structural reasons.

93
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.700
C++ dictates that struct memory maps must be concrete

94
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.200
and explicitly sized at compile time.

95
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.200
Dynamic generic parameters cannot map to a static physical size.

96
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.700
Union types present another boundary issue.

97
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.700
The common pattern of declaring a value as number or null

98
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.200
will fail code-gen validation immediately.

99
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.000
In C++, primitive numeric types do not possess

100
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.500
a null state representation at the memory level.

101
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.300
A double primitive simply cannot represent a null state.

102
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.500
Map and set structures are also unsupported.

103
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.800
Having no direct JSI equivalent,

104
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.100
they require standard objects or arrays

105
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.600
paired with native deduplication logic.

106
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.300
If you need a nullable number,

107
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.700
you must use an explicit object wrapper,

108
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.200
such as an object containing a numeric value or null.

109
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.900
These code-gen restrictions are not

110
00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.000
arbitrary TypeScript-linting rules.

111
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.000
They define the rigid physical boundaries

112
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.800
of static C++ memory allocation.

113
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.400
For asynchronous communication, the baseline rule

114
00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:04.600
is straightforward.

115
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.400
Prefer promises and avoid callbacks entirely

116
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.600
unless wrapping a specific native API

117
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.600
makes a promise unjustifiable.

118
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.100
Callbacks carry a strict native restriction.

119
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.800
They cannot be invoked more than once

120
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.200
without generating a completely new native invocation

121
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.700
from the JavaScript side.

122
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.900
For recurring events, react Native 0.76

123
00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.500
introduces a new spec-level emitter.

124
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.300
As you can see in this code comparison,

125
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.600
the legacy manual patterns are replaced.

126
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.700
This single-type definition eliminates

127
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.100
the error-prone setup previously required

128
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.300
by the old device emitter pattern.

129
00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.300
By defining the emitter in your spec,

130
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.800
code-gen automatically generates strictly-typed

131
00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.400
event subscription code natively

132
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.600
for both iOS and Android.

133
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.200
Standardizing around promises for single returns

134
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.500
and the new emitter for recurring streams

135
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.000
creates a statically-typed asynchronous pipeline

136
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.200
across the boundary.

137
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.700
We established earlier that the heavy asynchronous

138
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.600
JSON parsing over the old bridge has been replaced.

139
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.700
While zero-copy transmission is achieved within JSI,

140
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.700
a minor serialization cost exists

141
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.700
at the architecture's extreme edge.

142
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.100
Translating C++ memory into OS-specific native structures

143
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.300
is unavoidable.

144
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.400
However, this localized overhead is mathematically negligible

145
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.900
compared to the old JSON-over-Q model.

146
00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.200
To leverage the new architecture's performance,

147
00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.500
stop viewing your turbo-module-type script files

148
00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.000
as standard JavaScript.

149
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.500
Start treating them as strict C++ header files in disguise.

